#
# OpenSSL QUIC: work-gated port tick (Phase A)
# ============================================
#
# Problem
# -------
# ossl_quic_port_subtick() serviced every channel on the listener on every
# engine tick. A channel tick processes queued RX, ticks TLS, checks timers,
# generates/flushes TX and runs stream GC. One arriving datagram or one due
# timer therefore cost O(connections), making an idle fleet quadratic.
#
# What it does
# ------------
# Only channels with pending work are serviced:
#
#   * an active-channel list holds channels touched by the demux RX path, by
#     application actions (SSL_write, stream reset/conclude, close, key update,
#     ping), or by a stream becoming active;
#   * a timer min-heap keyed by each channel's cached next tick deadline finds
#     channels whose timers have expired in O(log N);
#   * the engine's next-wake deadline is derived from the earliest cached
#     channel deadline;
#   * handshaking channels remain scheduled even without network input, so
#     application-driven progress (for example a certificate-verification
#     retry) is preserved;
#   * if the timer heap cannot be allocated, the port falls back to the old
#     full channel scan;
#   * the channel tick gets a cheap "nothing to do" fast path guarded by
#     pending QRX work, queued QTX datagrams, stream-map work, the cached
#     deadline and the handshake state.
#
# No public API, ABI or configuration change is introduced.
#
# Files
# -----
#   include/internal/quic_channel.h
#   include/internal/quic_port.h
#   include/internal/quic_stream_map.h
#   ssl/quic/quic_channel.c
#   ssl/quic/quic_channel_local.h
#   ssl/quic/quic_port.c
#   ssl/quic/quic_port_local.h
#   ssl/quic/quic_stream_map.c
#
